WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.940
Laurie heard the beating of a drum coming from the study.

2
00:00:05.941 --> 00:00:11.711
Then she heard Grandpa call out, "Hear me, people, for I speak the truth!

3
00:00:11.712 --> 00:00:19.607
Tell this story to your children so the name Sundiata—the Lion King—lives forever!"

4
00:00:19.608 --> 00:00:26.842
"Grandpa!" Laurie pushed the study door open. "What are you doing?"

5
00:00:26.843 --> 00:00:30.442
Grandpa sat on the couch with a large drum.

6
00:00:30.443 --> 00:00:35.182
A stringed instrument was by his feet.

7
00:00:35.183 --> 00:00:39.821
"I'm practicing for my African storytelling performance.

8
00:00:39.822 --> 00:00:45.487
Hayside Elementary asked me to give a presentation to their third grade.

9
00:00:45.488 --> 00:00:50.699
I'll be telling them about the golden age of Africa."

10
00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:54.856
"But what are the instruments for?" Laurie asked.

11
00:00:54.857 --> 00:01:00.622
"African storytellers play instruments while sharing their legends and histories.

12
00:01:00.623 --> 00:01:05.578
It's usually this instrument called the kora and maybe a drum."

13
00:01:05.579 --> 00:01:13.252
"That's cool. What's the story about?" Laurie asked.

14
00:01:13.253 --> 00:01:20.506
"It's about three kingdoms of Africa—the Ghana, Mali, and Songhai kingdoms.

15
00:01:20.507 --> 00:01:26.052
These developed on the edge of the Sahara Desert.

16
00:01:26.053 --> 00:01:31.672
During the Middle Ages, some of the richest kingdoms in the world developed there.

17
00:01:31.673 --> 00:01:43.473
Originally, people of the Sahara were desert nomads who wandered from oasis to oasis to find water and vegetation for themselves and their herds.

18
00:01:43.474 --> 00:01:53.558
But legend says they always returned to one oasis, where they left their heavy belongings with a woman named Buktu.

19
00:01:53.559 --> 00:01:59.920
Eventually this spot became a city called Timbuktu, named after the woman.

20
00:01:59.921 --> 00:02:12.482
By around 1100 AD, Timbuktu was a major city along the trading route that crossed the Sahara and carried goods from Africa to

21
00:02:12.483 --> 00:02:19.226
Arabia." "Isn't the Sahara Desert huge?" Laurie asked. "How did they cross

22
00:02:19.227 --> 00:02:30.860
it?" "It is huge. Imagine that you have to journey through it for days and nights carrying loads of gold, fabric, iron tools, and clay

23
00:02:30.861 --> 00:02:35.727
pots. What would you do?" Grandpa paused and looked at

24
00:02:35.728 --> 00:02:42.412
Laurie. "Gather up your camels, of course! The Africans traveled by camel

25
00:02:42.413 --> 00:02:48.759
caravan. These animals can walk through hot sand for days without food or

26
00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:55.829
water. So traders loaded up about a hundred camels with goods and then set off into the

27
00:02:55.830 --> 00:03:00.243
desert. Usually the trip to Arabia took about forty

28
00:03:00.244 --> 00:03:09.120
days. "Can you guess what made the Africans so rich during their golden age?" Grandpa asked with a

29
00:03:09.121 --> 00:03:12.365
smile. "Gold!" Laurie

30
00:03:12.366 --> 00:03:24.572
responded. "Right! Gold and . . . salt," Grandpa said. "Salt was so important back then that it had the same value as

31
00:03:24.573 --> 00:03:31.730
gold. Salt is used to keep food from spoiling. It also makes food taste

32
00:03:31.731 --> 00:03:38.494
good. But most importantly it is essential to human bodies. We can't live without

33
00:03:38.495 --> 00:03:48.574
salt! And when you sweat, you lose a lot of salt, so it was especially important to Africans who lived in a hot climate and sweated a lot every

34
00:03:48.575 --> 00:03:57.119
day. "Salt and gold helped the first great African kingdom—the Ghana kingdom—come to power.

35
00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:02.931
Ghana's cities became home to the best artists and scholars on the continent.

36
00:04:02.932 --> 00:04:08.969
But eventually the Ghanaians were overrun by a group of Islamic Arabs.

37
00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:14.818
Wars broke out as different groups tried to gain control over the cities.

38
00:04:14.819 --> 00:04:23.779
Then a brave warrior named Sundiata, or 'hungering lion,' led his people to defeat the existing rulers.

39
00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:34.783
In 1235 he established the next great kingdom—the Mali kingdom—and created a stable government with a tax system. Under

40
00:04:34.784 --> 00:04:40.200
his rule, Mali grew richer and stronger every year. Then

41
00:04:40.201 --> 00:04:47.982
in 1307 another great leader, named Mansa Musa, came to the throne. He

42
00:04:47.983 --> 00:04:52.780
doubled the size of the kingdom and tripled its trade. Gold

43
00:04:52.781 --> 00:04:59.080
was flowing from Mali to Arabia, and then into Europe. "Islamic

44
00:04:59.081 --> 00:05:11.188
Arabic traders had been trying to convert Africans to Islam for years, and Mali was the first kingdom to accept Islam as its religion. In

45
00:05:11.189 --> 00:05:21.522
1324 Mansa Musa, a devoted Muslim, made a famous pilgrimage to Mecca, the birthplace of Islam. This

46
00:05:21.523 --> 00:05:27.511
pilgrimage gave Arabs their first image of how rich Mali was. Mansa

47
00:05:27.512 --> 00:05:34.014
Musa's caravan was the largest ever to travel across the Sahara. Five-hundred

48
00:05:34.015 --> 00:05:38.815
slaves walked before the great king. Mansa

49
00:05:38.816 --> 00:05:44.835
Musa, with his party of over fifty-thousand people, followed. One-hundred

50
00:05:44.836 --> 00:05:51.867
camels walked behind them, carrying over thirty-thousand pounds of gold. Mansa

51
00:05:51.868 --> 00:05:57.243
Musa was proud to present the riches of his kingdom to the Arabs. Everywhere

52
00:05:57.244 --> 00:06:01.535
he went he gave out beautiful gifts. He

53
00:06:01.536 --> 00:06:07.216
also went to markets and bought everything the merchants offered him. And

54
00:06:07.217 --> 00:06:15.388
he always paid in pure gold. Mansa Musa left quite an impression in Arabia. News

55
00:06:15.389 --> 00:06:25.542
of his incredible pilgrimage even traveled to Europe, where people were stunned by this rich king of Africa."

56
00:06:25.543 --> 00:06:30.492
"Did it make Europeans want to go to Africa too?" Laurie asked.

57
00:06:30.493 --> 00:06:34.510
"It did. But we'll get to that later.

58
00:06:34.511 --> 00:06:41.849
Still one more great African kingdom arose after Mali—the Songhai kingdom.

59
00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:49.935
Songhai was a territory that had been captured by Mali, and its people wanted their freedom back.

60
00:06:49.936 --> 00:07:02.135
In 1468 a Songhai leader named Sunni Ali Ber took over Timbuktu and gained control of the Saharan trading routes.

61
00:07:02.136 --> 00:07:08.945
The Mali kingdom fell apart, and the Songhai kingdom ruled West Africa.

62
00:07:08.946 --> 00:07:19.577
"But no golden age lasts forever. In 1591 Muslims from Morocco attacked the Songhai kingdom.

63
00:07:19.578 --> 00:07:29.044
And you were absolutely right, Laurie. Learning about the rich African kingdoms made people want to take control of Africa.

64
00:07:29.045 --> 00:07:39.490
In the 1600s the Portuguese and Dutch began setting up their own cities and colonies there. But that's another story."

65
00:07:39.491 --> 00:07:45.416
"Wow, I never knew Africa had kingdoms like that," Laurie said.

66
00:07:45.417 --> 00:07:55.297
"Yeah," Grandpa said sadly. "Most people only learn about the slave trade that brought Africans to the Americas.

67
00:07:55.298 --> 00:08:02.834
But Africans have a much greater history to be proud of.

68
00:08:02.835 --> 00:08:08.537
And I will be proud to tell it." Grandpa tapped his drum.

69
00:08:08.538 --> 00:08:12.398
"Well, I have a great story of my own to tell."

70
00:08:12.399 --> 00:08:16.309
Laurie moved the drum between her legs and tapped it.

71
00:08:16.310 --> 00:08:22.794
"Listen to my story. Here are the adventures of a fourth-grader!"

72
00:08:22.795 --> 00:08:35.592
And she began to tell Grandpa about a brave young girl named Laurie and her wise grandfather who studied the past.

